{{also|to-night|tonite|to-nite}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|to-night}} {{qualifier|archaic}}
* {{l|en|to-nite}} {{qualifier|US|informal|archaic}}
* {{l|en|tonite}} {{qualifier|US|informal}}
* {{l|en|t'night}} {{qualifier|US|informal}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|tonyght}}, {{m|enm|to niȝt}}, from {{inh|en|ang|tō niht}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/təˈnaɪt/}}
* {{IPA|en|/ðəˈniːt/|a=Northumbrian}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-tonight.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪt|s=2}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-}}

# During the [[night]] following the current [[day]]; during the [[evening]] of today.
#: {{ux|en|I want to party '''tonight'''!}}
#: {{ux|en|I had a wonderful time with you '''tonight'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|I had a dreadful nightmare last night; I hope I sleep better '''tonight'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} [[last night|Last night]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1596|author=w:William Shakespeare|title=s:The Life and Death of King John|section=act 4, scene 2|page=165|text=Besides, I met Lord Bigot and Lord Salisbury,<br>With eyes as red as new-enkindled fire,<br>And others more, going to seek the grave<br>Of Arthur, whom they say is killed '''to-night'''<br>On your suggestion.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|3|3|text=I dreamt '''tonight''' that I did feast with Caesar,<br>And things unluckily charge my fantasy;}}

